WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.440
Culture shows how the program runs.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.980
What happens in a whole animal when you delete its parts?

3
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.173
Delete caspase-3, or caspase-9, from a mouse.

4
00:00:13.993 --> 00:00:17.353
Do the spinal and peripheral neurons that normally die survive?

5
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:26.973
No. Oppenheim's group counted them.

6
00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:29.733
The same number died as in normal mice.

7
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:33.887
But the deaths came later, and they did not look apoptotic.

8
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.697
Chromatin condensation was absent or reduced.

9
00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:40.557
The cytoplasm filled with vacuoles.

10
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.620
So deleting a caspase changes how these neurons die.

11
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:47.143
Now delete Bax instead.

12
00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:52.403
In many neuron populations, naturally occurring death was largely eliminated.

13
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:56.923
Bax deletion does not rescue every developing neuron.

14
00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:00.823
But where neurons depend on Bax, deleting it stops the death.

15
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:04.797
In those populations, caspases decide how.

16
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:06.817
Bax decides whether.

17
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.690
The saved neurons were not normal.

18
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.440
In the spinal nerve roots and the facial nerve, myelinated axon counts rose by about

19
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.700
30 to 55 percent. Many extra axons were very small.

20
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:25.403
Extra spinal motor neurons began to innervate their targets.

21
00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:28.283
Then a subset shrank, and lost contact.

22
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:32.743
A neuron saved from death may survive as a smaller cell.

23
00:01:33.563 --> 00:01:35.183
That fits too little target support.

24
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:39.543
Mice lacking NGF lose their sympathetic neurons.

25
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:42.923
So you cannot ask what else NGF does for them.

26
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:48.487
In mice lacking both NGF and Bax, the sympathetic neurons survive.

27
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.590
If NGF did nothing but keep neurons alive, what would their innervation of organs

28
00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:55.590
look like?

29
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:04.883
It would look like the innervation in mice lacking only Bax.

30
00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:06.763
It did not.

31
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:12.267
The axons failed to fully innervate many targets, and the failure differed by organ.

32
00:02:13.317 --> 00:02:16.017
The salivary glands had none, or almost none.

33
00:02:16.877 --> 00:02:18.237
The heart was greatly reduced.

34
00:02:19.137 --> 00:02:20.197
The trachea was normal.

35
00:02:21.217 --> 00:02:26.757
So NGF is needed for axons to grow into many targets, as well as for survival.

36
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.510
Here is the general logic.

37
00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.710
Delete the death machinery, and look at what is still wrong once dying is no longer

38
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:35.710
an option.

39
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.530
The deleted gene may have other jobs too, so read such results with care.

40
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.570
The killers are already built; a survival signal keeps brakes on them.

41
00:02:46.483 --> 00:02:49.263
Brake one is the gate at the mitochondrion.

42
00:02:49.703 --> 00:02:52.423
NGF loss opens it through new gene expression.

43
00:02:53.517 --> 00:02:57.877
Brake two is on the caspases, and deprivation lifts it without new protein.

44
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.630
The neuron dies by apoptosis only when both brakes are off.

45
00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:08.603
Which neurons get enough NGF in the first place is a question about targets, and

46
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:09.343
about competition.
